---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const phases = [
  {
    n: 1,
    name: 'Ask',
    question: 'What is the real problem, and whose is it?',
    output: 'A business question, the decision it unlocks, and metrics with operational definitions.',
  },
  {
    n: 2,
    name: 'Prepare',
    question: 'Is this data any good, and can I trust it?',
    output: 'A record for every source: origin, licence, period, known biases.',
  },
  {
    n: 3,
    name: 'Process',
    question: 'Is it clean, and can I prove it?',
    output: 'A cleaning log where every transformation carries its reason and its row counts.',
  },
  {
    n: 4,
    name: 'Analyse',
    question: 'What does the data actually say?',
    output: 'Results, plus the checks I ran to try to break them.',
  },
  {
    n: 5,
    name: 'Share',
    question: 'Will the audience understand it?',
    output: 'Charts whose headline is the finding, not the variable name.',
  },
  {
    n: 6,
    name: 'Act',
    question: 'What should the business do?',
    output: 'Recommendations ranked by impact against effort, with their limits stated.',
  },
];

const layers = [
  {
    name: 'The card',
    time: '15 seconds',
    what: 'Headline, key chart, and what the case demonstrates. The home page.',
  },
  {
    name: 'The case',
    time: '5 minutes',
    what: 'Context, data, process, findings, recommendations, limitations.',
  },
  {
    name: 'The evidence',
    time: '30 minutes',
    what: 'Cleaning log, scripts, source records, the full case record. In the repository.',
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Method"
  description="How I run a data analysis case study: six phases, each with an exit gate that has to be passed before the next one starts."
>
  <article class="shell method">
    <p class="eyebrow">Method</p>
    <h1>How I work</h1>

    <p class="lede">
      Every case on this site follows the same six phases, adapted from the Google Data Analytics
      process. What I added is the part that makes it repeatable: each phase has an exit gate.
    </p>

    <p>
      I do not move to the next phase until every item on that gate is met. When something fails, I
      go back rather than improvise forward — finding out during analysis that the data cannot
      answer the question means returning to Ask, not writing a softer conclusion.
    </p>

    <h2>The six phases</h2>

    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>#</th>
            <th>Phase</th>
            <th>Question it answers</th>
            <th>What it produces</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            phases.map((p) => (
              <tr>
                <td class="num">{p.n}</td>
                <td><strong>{p.name}</strong></td>
                <td>{p.question}</td>
                <td>{p.output}</td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Three rules I do not break</h2>

    <p>
      <strong>Everything is documented as it happens, not afterwards.</strong> In three of the six
      phases the documentation <em>is</em> the deliverable. Reconstructing decisions from memory
      produces weak case studies, and that is exactly the detail an experienced reviewer notices.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>No invented numbers.</strong> If a figure did not come out of the data, it does not
      enter the report. Where there is a gap, it is declared as a limitation.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Raw data is never edited in place.</strong> Every transformation produces a new file
      and an entry in the cleaning log, so the whole chain can be re-run from the original source.
    </p>

    <h2>What you can read, and how deep</h2>

    <p>
      A case study produces far more material than anyone wants to read at once, so it is published
      in three layers. This page and the case pages are the first two; the third is one click away
      in each case's repository, for anyone who wants to check the work rather than take my word
      for it.
    </p>

    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Layer</th>
            <th>Time</th>
            <th>What it is</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            layers.map((l) => (
              <tr>
                <td><strong>{l.name}</strong></td>
                <td class="nowrap">{l.time}</td>
                <td>{l.what}</td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>How I choose what to work on next</h2>

    <p>
      A portfolio is an argument about what you can do, not a folder of projects — three cases
      demonstrating different things beat eight demonstrating the same one. So before starting a
      case I check what it would add along four axes: the <strong>type of problem</strong>, the{' '}
      <strong>main tool</strong>, the <strong>kind of data</strong>, and whether the result has ever
      contradicted my starting assumption. If a candidate case fills none of those gaps, the effort
      is better spent elsewhere.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  h1 {
    margin: var(--space-s) 0 var(--space-m);
  }

  /* El texto mantiene su medida; las tablas ocupan todo el ancho del shell,
     que es bastante más. */
  .method > p,
  .method > h2 {
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .table-wrap {
    overflow-x: auto;
    margin-block: var(--space-m) var(--space-l);
  }

  .num {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .nowrap {
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
</style>
